#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055897} :activity/id #uuid "1741802a-ec50-5bf7-99b5-64889f83ba7d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714 :topic/id #uuid "0d37b7c6-5217-5761-9584-b301738c5f16" :topic/math-academy-id 125} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056006 :tutorial/content "We've already seen that $5^2$ means $5\\cdot 5$, and that $5^3$ means $5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5$. We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{copies of} 5}\n$$\n\nThe **exponent** $({\\color{blue}7})$ says how many times the **base** $(5)$ is multiplied by itself.\n\nRecall that $5^2$ is said as \"five squared\" and that $5^3$ is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say $5^7$ in several different ways:\n\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, $5^{\\color{blue}1}$ means one copy of $5$, so\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "964b1381-44c4-5a32-832a-ec441cc4a890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8020 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bb689754-aa8e-5f1a-bf8d-0c16cbd2123a" :step/math-academy-id 74297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054371 :question/id #uuid "890b54a1-db53-5a05-bdba-1477aed3461a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40e41bd9-1eee-552d-8507-e8cdf50ddfa4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055097} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860